In NET, C1 is equidistant to D1 and D2 (both road distance 4: D1->X->C1 = 2+2, D2->C1 = 4). C4 is also equidistant (D1->C4 = 5, D2->C4 = 5).

The C1 assertions require the tie-break to be independent of depot order and to pick D2 over D1 (i.e. lexicographically-greater depot wins). That same rule yields C4->D2, not C4->D1. No single deterministic rule (order-independence is required by the two C1 cases) satisfies both. The C4 expectation appears to be a test bug. Please confirm the intended tie-break and fix the C4 assertion (should be D2 under lex-greater). Implementation in assign_depots.py follows lex-greater and is otherwise green.

